  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1208 AM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nOur area will be under northwest flow aloft on Wednesday, with an \nupper level ridge over the Intermountain West, and an upper trough \ndeveloping over the Great Lakes and adjacent Midwest. With this \nsetup a weak, dry cold front will move south across our area \nduring the day Wednesday. Winds will shift to the north with this \nfrontal passage but wind speeds will be just 5-10 mph. After \nhighs in the mid to upper 70s Wednesday, temperatures will only be \na few degrees cooler on Thursday. Skies will be sunny both \nWednesday and Thursday. Our area will have a couple of cooler \nnights Wednesday night and Thursday night. Clear skies, light \nwinds and radiational cooling should allow temperatures to drop \nmostly into the 40s for lows. Friday looks pleasant with mostly \nsunny skies, a return to south winds and highs in the mid to upper\n70s. A weakening upper ridge will shift east across Texas on \nFriday. \n\nOur next rain chance will be this next weekend, mainly on Saturday \ninto Saturday night. An upper shortwave trough is forecast to move \nfrom New Mexico and northern Mexico east across our area on \nSaturday, while another shortwave trough moves east across the \ncentral Rockies into Kansas and Oklahoma on Sunday. Moisture will \nquickly increase with the approach and arrival of the New \nMexico/northern Mexico trough. This system will bring a low to \nmedium chance (20-50 percent) chance of showers to our area \nSaturday into Saturday night. With increased cloud cover, \ntemperatures will be cooler Saturday and highs should be in the \nmid to upper 60s for much of our area. With passage of the second \naforementioned upper trough across Kansas and Oklahoma, a cold \nfront is progged to push south across our area Sunday night into \nMonday morning. North winds to follow passage of this cold front \nwith temperatures several degrees cooler for Monday.",
